  - title: "Keynote - Agent Smith Is in Your Stack: A Practical Guide to Governing AI Integration Before It Governs You"
    description: "AI didn't ask permission before it moved into your organisation. It's in your codebase as a copilot, in your SaaS tools as a hidden feature, on your vendors' roadmaps and increasingly it acts on its own: reading data, calling APIs, making decisions. Most security programmes are still governing yesterday's threat model."
    speakers:
      - Susanne Bitter
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T08:05:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T08:45:00Z"
    tracks: 1
    room: ~
  - title: "The Silence of the LAMs: Detecting Rogue Language Models"
    description: "First principles: the most dangerous threats in your enterprise aren't the ones making noise. They're the ones you've already let in.\nShadow AI: the use of unapproved AI tools, models, and services without IT or security oversight  is now one of the fastest-growing sources of uncontrolled data exposure in organisations of every size. Microsoft research confirms that 71% of UK employees have used unapproved consumer AI tools at work, with 51% doing so every single week. Most security teams have no visibility whatsoever. The transaction is frictionless, the blast radius is enormous, and the perpetrator is probably your most productive employee.\nThis talk is structured around the three questions: where is it hiding, how do you find it, and what do you do when you finally locate it in the dark?\nWhere is it hiding? We'll map the Shadow AI attack surface: public LLM APIs called directly from corporate devices, AI-powered browser extensions intercepting clipboard and page content, local models running entirely on-device to evade network controls, agentic automation workflows quietly piping internal data to external inference endpoints, and AI features embedded inside approved SaaS tools with their own opaque data handling.\nHow do you find it? We'll look at detection across the stack. At the network layer: DNS monitoring against a continuously updated AI domain blocklist, TLS SNI inspection without decryption, and NetFlow anomaly detection for large sustained POST volumes. At the endpoint: EDR telemetry for local LLM runtimes, model weight file signatures, and browser extension enumeration. At the identity layer: OAuth consent grant analysis, SSO bypass detection, and personal account usage on corporate devices. Tying it all together with SIEM correlation rules that build high-fidelity Shadow AI alerts from low-fidelity signals.\nWhat do you do when you find it? Here's where most organisations struggle. Blocking doesn't work, it just drives the behaviour underground, onto mobile hotspots and personal laptops. We'll cover incident response for Shadow AI exposure events, how to build an AI acceptable use policy employees will actually follow, and how to construct an approved AI programme that removes the incentive to go rogue in the first place.\nAttendees will leave with an idea for a detection playbook, practical SIEM rules they can implement the following Monday, and a slightly unsettling awareness of what my be lurking in the dark.\n\"You still wake up sometimes, don't you? Wake up in the dark?\" Maybe hopefully you can sleep soundly knowing you have a plan to stop the lams screaming."
    speakers:
      - Tasha Armstrong-Godwin
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T09:00:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T09:40:00Z"
    tracks: 1
    room: ~
  - title: How Resilient is your MFA?
    description: "Quite often, we go to the effort of backing up our documents and photos, but have you ever considered what you would do if your phone decided to pack up one day?\n\nFrom a security perspective, in this day and age, our mobile phones have become even more important than our main devices (PCs, laptops), with our passkeys, MFA, etc., stored on them.\n\nSo it begs the question, how redundant are our MFA implementations and is this something we should perhaps pay a little more attention to?\n\nIn this talk, we explore why we need MFA redundancy, what MFA redundancy looks like, and how we should go about implementing it."
    speakers:
      - Shammas H (@CyberShams)
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T09:00:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T09:20:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: "Beyond the Air Gap: How IT/OT Convergence Changes the Attack Surface"
    description: "Operational Technology networks are often discussed as if they are completely isolated. In reality, remote maintenance, vendor access, business systems and centralised monitoring have created more connections between IT and OT.\n\nThis talk explains how that convergence changes the attack surface and why familiar networking weaknesses have different consequences when the network controls a physical process.\n\nI will walk through a simple attack path from remote access into an OT environment, introducing concepts such as segmentation, trust boundaries, HMIs, PLCs and RTUs along the way. I will also briefly reference real incidents affecting European energy infrastructure to show how network access can move towards operational disruption.\n\nThis is an introduction to modern OT networking, focused on one question: how can an ordinary network weakness become a physical problem?"
    speakers:
      - Akash
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T09:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T09:50:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: "You’ve Been Ph0wned: How Attackers Compromise Organisations Via Telephone Social Engineering"
    description: "Modern vishing attacks exploit the tension between fast, helpful support and rigorous identity verification, enabling a single convincing phone call to bypass even strong technical controls. \nDrawing on real-world red team engagements and a live AI voice-cloning demonstration, this session examines the full attack lifecycle - from reconnaissance and pretext development to human manipulation and account compromise. Attendees will gain practical guidance for planning safe, ethical and effective vishing simulations, including actionable lessons and common pitfalls encountered in the field."
    speakers:
      - Luiz Simpson
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T10:00:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T10:40:00Z"
    tracks: 1
    room: ~
  - title: "Hooked: Investigating the Phishing Attack That Targeted Me at University"
    description: "Phishing emails are everywhere. But what happens when you are the target? This presentation is about a real life phishing incident I experienced as a student at London South Bank University and the findings I uncovered during my investigation.I noticed some suspicious activity and rather than just delete it, I thought I’d dig a little deeper. I’m quite proud of what I uncovered about the spoofed sender, the social engineering tricks they employed and the infrastructure behind it all - which I reported to the university IT team.In this discussion we will cover how the phishing attack bypassed SPF, DKIM and DMARC, the investigation process using open source tools, subtle red flags that may be overlooked, and practical tips to detect and respond to phishing attacks."
    speakers:
      - Prithika Gopinath
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T10:00:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T10:20:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: "How Not to Roll Out a Security Tool: A Tale of Broken PRs and Regret"
    description: "In DevSecOps, we all love a vendor shootout. Which scanner has the best coverage? Which dashboard is the slickest? Which tool flags the most CVEs?\n\nBut the truth is that rolling out a security tool is 20% getting the invoice paid and 80% communication and desperately trying not to break everyone’s builds. Get that 80% wrong, and you don’t get security. You just get developers figuring out how to bypass you.\n\nThis talk is a post-mortem of an easy mistake from early in my career. I dropped a shiny new software composition analysis (SCA) tool straight into our CI/CD pipeline before I’d tuned the noise, agreed on a rollout model, or given developers a sensible way to respond. For a few newer microservices, it was an inconvenience that could be fixed, but for our core legacy monolith? It was a self-inflicted denial-of-service on delivery.\n\nPRs backed up. Releases stalled. Trust evaporated, and security instantly became the dreaded “Department of No.” Ultimately, I had to turn the tool off for the monolith so we could get releases out the door.\n\nThe lesson was brutal but simple: I hadn’t secured the pipeline. I had just trained the engineering team to route around me.\n\nWe’ll break down exactly what went wrong and how to fix it. We’ll look at how to roll out tooling without the fallout, how to separate visibility from enforcement, and how to introduce blocking controls that engineers actually respect."
    speakers:
      - James
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T10:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T10:50:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: "Ghost in the Kernel: Hunting BYOVD After Microsoft’s 2026 Driver Trust Shift"
    description: "BYOVD attacks have helped ransomware crews and advanced actors disable security tools, hide activity, and move from admin control towards the kernel. Microsoft’s 2026 Windows Driver Policy is a major hardening step, but it does not make kernel-driver risk disappear. This talk explains what the new policy blocks, what it only audits, and what defenders still need to hunt. Using cases including BlackByte, POORTRY/STONESTOP, and Lazarus/FudModule, we will build a practical approach to driver inventory, Code Integrity events, audit-mode visibility, and reducing kernel attack surface."
    speakers:
      - Richard Dosumu
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T12:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T13:10:00Z"
    tracks: 1
    room: ~
  - title: Honeypot detection through machine wear and tear
    description: "Honeypots, which are a high-fidelity intelligence gathering strategy to detect, delay or study adversarial behaviour by simulating vulnerable targets, face the challenge of irrelevancy if adversaries are able to detect and therefore avoid them. Previous research has observed that external behaviours of fake headers and unconvincing error responses lead to detection, but this presentation argues that it is the absent inevitable ageing through day to day use that gives the game away. \n\nThe SANS ISC DShield honeypot was deployed in the cloud and systematically tested against a taxonomy of “wear and tear” across disk, registry, system, browser and network artefacts to assess the level of configuration of the honeypot. Logs from a 30-day period were collected and analysed to identify if adversaries were employing techniques of examining system usage within their attack paths. \n\nIt was observed that when DShield is deployed “off the shelf” it has limited configuration of wear and tear artefacts which reveal the true nature of the machine. 78% of artefacts tested were configured to an unsatisfactory level or absent altogether. This presentation highlights how honeypot operators need to further configure honeypots in order to create a more convincing decoy."
    speakers:
      - Matilda Moore
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T12:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T12:50:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: ADS-B and Aviation Security
    description: "ADS-B is one of the technologies at the forefront of aviation safety, broadcasting information from planes to the ground and other aircraft alerting them of their position, speed and heading. The protocal used is entirely unauthenticated and unencrypted, and malicious actors are able to easily inject false information into this system. Based on my undergradutate dissertation, this talk will discuss the development of ADS-B, the security issues that it has, and the work being done to counteract these problems."
    speakers:
      - Oliver Bouchier
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T13:00:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T13:20:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: "Breaching The Perimeter: The Forgotten Attack Vector That Always Works"
    description: "If you can open the server room door, you don’t need exploits.\n\nIn this talk, we demonstrate nine real-world ways attackers bypass a server room door and achieve full compromise—no malware, no zero-days, no phishing required. Firewalls, EDR, and IAM become irrelevant the moment physical access is gained.\n\nThis is not theory. These are techniques used in actual red team engagements across Europe. We show how attackers exploit trust, abuse operational gaps, and chain physical access into full compromise. These techniques go beyond tailgating.\n\nWe also cover how modern attackers accelerate these intrusions using AI—automating OSINT to map targets and using deepfake voice pretexting to convincingly talk their way through restricted access points.\n\nIf your threat model stops at the network edge, this talk will break it."
    speakers:
      - tatramaco
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T13:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T14:10:00Z"
    tracks: 1
    room: ~
  - title: "We Didn't Have a Patch: How Defence in Depth Saved Our Customers from Log4Shell"
    description: "This isn't really a talk about Log4Shell. It's the story of what happened when a global zero-day collided with a real customer network — and what we discovered when we went back through the evidence years later.\n\nIn December 2021, a customer called to tell us they'd been hit by Log4Shell. Their application server had been behaving badly, disks were filling, services were failing, and the timing appeared to fit.\nBut did Log4Shell actually cause it?\n\nUsing anonymised evidence from the incident — application logs, emails, firewall configuration and the original customer timeline — I'll reconstruct what we saw at the time, what the rest of the world was seeing, and what the surviving evidence now tells us actually happened.\n\nAlong the way we'll see genuine Log4Shell traffic reach the customer's application and examine the independent security layers surrounding it.\n\nThe important part of the story isn't a particular firewall, product or CVE. It's that security decisions made years earlier were already limiting what an attacker could do before anybody knew what Log4Shell was — and before a patch was available.\n\nThe technology has changed since 2021. The principles haven't."
    speakers:
      - Simon wilks
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T13:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T13:50:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: Love You! Now Please Buy Me Gift Cards
    description: "Your online soulmate just needs one small favour: your bank account. AI now writes the messages, clones the voices and generates the faces while the attackers sit back and watch. Romance scams have stopped looking like scams. They look like real people in relationships. So how do you tell the difference between social engineering and love when everything just feels so real?"
    speakers:
      - Aurelia Brzezowska
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T14:00:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T14:20:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: "So You Want To Get Into Red Teaming: Skills, Mindset, and Myths"
    description: "Red teaming is often portrayed as a niche, ultra-advanced discipline reserved only for experts with deep exploit-development skills and expensive toolsets. \n\nIn reality, effective red teaming is built on fundamentals: understanding enterprise environments, thinking in attack paths, communicating clearly, working within realistic constraints and problem solving. \n\nThis talk is designed for people who want to enter the field and need a practical view of what red teaming actually involves, what skills matter most at the start, and which common myths can slow progress and distract you from real goals. \n\nI’ll separate hype from reality, outline the core technical and non-technical skills that make a strong red teamer, and offer concrete ways to begin building experience constructively and effectively."
    speakers:
      - Chris Pritchard
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T14:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T15:10:00Z"
    tracks: 1
    room: ~
  - title: "Your Daily Routine, Their Daily Recon"
    description: "In today’s digital landscape, “day-in-the-life” content has grown significantly in popularity, with individuals sharing detailed insights into their daily routines across social media platforms. Whilst often perceived as harmless, this content can reveal far more than intended.\n\nThis talk adopts an adversary's perspective to demonstrate how short, informal videos can be analysed to build detailed intelligence on both the individuals and the businesses they represent.\n\nDrawing on real-world case studies, the session will show how seemingly minor visual and contextual details can expose information about home environments, workplaces, routines, security practices, and operational processes.\n\nIt highlights how low-risk content can quickly become a valuable intelligence source and outlines how organisations can better understand and mitigate this evolving exposure."
    speakers:
      - George-Octavian Vieru
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T14:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T14:50:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: "Still Supported Isn't the Same as Still Safe: Legacy Assets and the Enforcement Gap"
    description: "Organisations keep running insecure legacy systems not because they don't know about the risk, but because replacing them costs more than they can afford. And even when a system is supported and the patch is free, there are laws and guidelines saying you must update but actually enforcing that across every system in a large organisation is a different problem entirely. This talk uses real incidents to show both failure modes, and ends with three things you can actually do about it this week."
    speakers:
      - Mateusz Madrzynski
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T15:00:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T15:20:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
  - title: Metrics that lie
    description: "A talk on how common SOC metrics can look accurate while misleading leadership, hiding operational reality, and driving the wrong business decisions."
    speakers:
      - Luigi Ritacca
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T15:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T15:50:00Z"
    tracks: 1
    room: ~
  - title: "Ghosts in the Sky: Creating Phantom Aircraft with Mode-S Transponder Spoofing"
    description: "Modern commercial aircraft constantly exchange information using Mode-S transponders, ADS-B broadcasts, and TCAS collision avoidance messages. These systems help keep aircraft safely separated, but were designed before authenticated wireless communications became a security concern.\n\nThis talk provides an introduction to the protocols that underpin airborne collision avoidance and investigates whether a phantom aircraft could be introduced into the ACAS surveillance process through maliciously crafted Mode-S transmissions."
    speakers:
      - Alex Carr
    start_utc: "2026-08-22T15:30:00Z"
    end_utc: "2026-08-22T15:50:00Z"
    tracks: 4
    room: ~
